{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1553089584,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NDgzNTc5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3206#issuecomment-474835795"},"message":"I was suffering from this too. I think we have an oportunity now to use `status` to make things more clever.\n\nWe get the results from get() hence every single input file. We can now resolve the output specification using status(). Only files in the intersection of the two sets need to be unlocked. Everything else can be os.remove()'d. Whatever doesn't match status() does not exist to begin with.\n\nThis avoids adding another argument.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553092500,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NDg1NzUwNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3206#issuecomment-474857504"},"message":"rm'ing outputs (which aren't inputs) might have undesired effects of wiping out outputs which aren't (re)created, and run recording changes as they were removed by the ran command.\nMy recent use case is running fmriprep on a subset of subjects.  So inputs was the original bids dataset, and outputs - well, the entirety of the fmriprep output, which already contained a number subjects.  Lazy/busy user me, would not specify all outputs one by one (in many cases it would not be even feasible if outputs are not having one clear subdirectory) - I just specify the entire output dataset.  I know that outputs would not chock on already committed previously outputs - they are either directly in git, or would be created a new, or rm'ed first by the script.  So, most logical option for me is to disable unlocking of the outputs.\n\nIn the light of the multi-stage proposal (#3162) we could allow to explicitly list or disable (with `-`), and may be even later on to enable (with `+`) some optional operations (procedures?).  E.g. in this case I would have specified `--stages=-unlock` or more explicit `--stages=-unlock-outputs`, if such a stage (`unlock` or `unlock-outputs`) was defined.  In some cases, to not waste time, I might say `--stages=-get-inputs` if I know that inputs are already present, and so on.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553092500,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5ODg4MjI1MQ=="},"target":"0d26d5a21e0a807b14b0d32293cf7a23c0b81c10894a9cf3f1267930fb0c2358","message":"rm'ing outputs (which aren't inputs) might have undesired effects of wiping out outputs which aren't (re)created, and run recording changes as they were removed by the ran command.\nMy recent use case is running fmriprep on a subset of subjects.  So inputs was the original bids dataset, and outputs - well, the entirety of the fmriprep output, which already contained a number subjects.  Lazy/busy user me, would not specify all outputs one by one (in many cases it would not be even feasible if outputs are not having one clear subdirectory) - I just specify the entire output dataset.  I know that outputs would not choke on already committed previously outputs - they are either directly in git, or would be created a new, or rm'ed first by the script, or they are explicitly unlocked in v7, or ...  So, most logical option for me is to disable unlocking of the outputs.\n\nIn the light of the multi-stage proposal (#3162) we could allow to explicitly list or disable (with `-`), and may be even later on to enable (with `+`) some optional operations (procedures?).  E.g. in this case I would have specified `--stages=-unlock` or more explicit `--stages=-unlock-outputs`, if such a stage (`unlock` or `unlock-outputs`) was defined.  In some cases, to not waste time, I might say `--stages=-get-inputs` if I know that inputs are already present, and so on.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553092526,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5ODg4MjUzNA=="},"target":"0d26d5a21e0a807b14b0d32293cf7a23c0b81c10894a9cf3f1267930fb0c2358","message":"rm'ing outputs (which aren't inputs) might have undesired effects of wiping out outputs which aren't (re)created, and run recording changes as they were removed by the ran command.\nMy recent use case is running fmriprep on a subset of subjects.  So inputs was the original bids dataset, and outputs - well, the entirety of the fmriprep output, which already contained a number subjects.  Lazy/busy user me, would not specify all outputs one by one (in many cases it would not be even feasible if outputs are not having one clear subdirectory) - I just specify the entire output dataset.  I know that outputs would not choke on already committed previously outputs - they are either directly in git, or would be created a new, or rm'ed first by the script, or they are explicitly unlocked in v7, or ...  So, most logical option for me is to disable unlocking of the outputs. If all non-input outputs were removed, I would loose all previous results.\n\nIn the light of the multi-stage proposal (#3162) we could allow to explicitly list or disable (with `-`), and may be even later on to enable (with `+`) some optional operations (procedures?).  E.g. in this case I would have specified `--stages=-unlock` or more explicit `--stages=-unlock-outputs`, if such a stage (`unlock` or `unlock-outputs`) was defined.  In some cases, to not waste time, I might say `--stages=-get-inputs` if I know that inputs are already present, and so on.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553484704,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NjA0MzM1MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3206#issuecomment-476043351"},"message":"and ran into this while preparing what I thought would be a smooth demo... until I got to this issue again - output file